<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ancient Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200 BCE – c. 600 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hellas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was a civilisation of culturally and linguistically related but independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and regions centred on the northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, lasting from the Greek Dark Ages of the 12th–9th centuries BCE to the end of classical antiquity (c. 600 CE). City-states, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, formed in the 8th century BCE, opening the Archaic period and Greek colonisation of the Mediterranean Basin. The Classical period, from the Greco-Persian Wars to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 323 BCE, included the Golden Age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the Peloponnesian War. Alexander's conquests spread Greek culture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, which ended c. 30 BCE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ancient-greece.jpg" alt="Ancient Greece" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phanatic · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parthenon_(30276156187).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Archaic period, from about 800 to 500 BCE, completed political developments begun in the Greek Dark Age. After the collapse of Mycenaean power the geography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, where mountains separated many settlements, encouraged small independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>. Tyrants rose in several of them, at Corinth from 657 BCE. Euboean settlers founded colonies at Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> in the east by 800 BCE and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> in the west by 775. Contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> inspired developments in art and architecture, the adoption of coinage and the development of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>.</p><p>In 499 BCE the Ionian city-states rebelled against Persian-supported tyrants and, with troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria, burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. In 490 the Athenians, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, defeated an armada sent by Darius at Marathon. Rome conquered the Greek peninsula in 146 BCE after the Battle of Corinth, and Augustus organised the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in 27 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> consisted of several hundred relatively independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, unlike most contemporary societies, which were tribal or large kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> regarded themselves as one people with the same religion, culture and language, but the independence of the poleis was fiercely defended. Even during the second Persian invasion most poleis stayed neutral, and after the Persian defeat the allies quickly returned to infighting.</p><p>Only free, land-owning, native-born men could be citizens. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the population was divided into four classes by wealth; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> all male citizens were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">homoioi</a>, &quot;peers&quot;, while the two kings came from two families. Education was private except in Sparta. Armies were made up of citizens, so campaigns were mostly confined to summer, and casualties rarely exceeded five per cent of the losing side. A pantheon of twelve Olympians began to develop as early as the 6th century BCE, and the most important religious act was animal sacrifice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Much of what is known comes from historians, most of them Athenian or pro-Athenian, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, whose Histories were written between the 450s and 420s BCE; their focus on political and military history leaves economic and social history thin. Greek mathematics produced the basic rules of geometry and the idea of formal proof. Greek culture became the Leitkultur of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> inherited it directly, which influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ancient Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Ancient Greece</b> pacifism seems not to have existed except as a broad moral guideline against violence between individuals.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Ancient Greece</b> a limited secularism was practised, in which religion was not involved in governance though it remained prevalent in public life.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/secularism">Secularism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
